# THE THIRD DOOR, AND THE ONE THE FLAG DID NOT OPEN UNTIL NOW.
#
# Its two siblings are test_nilpy_import_zlib.npy (explicit extension -> the C
# header) and test_nilpy_import_zlib_ours.npy (bare name -> lib/rtl/zlib.pas).
# This row is the SAME SOURCE as neither and the same SPELLING as the second:
# a bare `import zlib`, compiled with --no-shims.
#
# A bare NilPy import has TWO ours-first substitutions in front of it, not one:
# a mimic_<name> shim, and a curated lib/rtl unit on PyRtlUnitServesPython.
# `--no-shims` used to lift only the first, so the flag meant "no shim" for the
# names a shim serves and "no shim, except that we substitute for this one by a
# different mechanism" for the seventeen on the list -- a distinction no caller
# can read out of the flag's name or its refusal text.
#
# compressBound is the right call BECAUSE OUR UNIT DOES NOT HAVE IT. It is a
# pure function of its argument (len + len>>12 + len>>14 + len>>25 + 13, so
# 1000 -> 1013 on every zlib ever shipped), and lib/rtl/zlib.pas offers the
# Python checksum surface and a pure-Pascal inflate, not zlib's C API. So this
# file WITHOUT the flag is not a different number -- it is
# `undefined variable (compressBound)`, which is what makes the row unable to
# pass if the flag does nothing. The Makefile asserts that refusal as the
# positive control, in the same recipe, rather than trusting that the two doors
# would have printed different values.
#
# AND THE DT_NEEDED HALF IS THE ASSERTION THAT CANNOT PASS BY ACCIDENT: reaching
# the header means linking libz.so.1, and _ours must carry NO dynamic dependency
# at all. The pair differs in exactly one thing, the flag.
import zlib
print(compressBound(1000))
